  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "8gffm4/qVisZhfjjUB+2RTnw74M=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:17.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "## Four questions, four owners\n\nFour concerns all meet at a single operation, and conflating them is the most common source of confusion about what a signature buys you:\n\n| Gate | Question | Where it lives |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Authentication** | Who is this identity? | Renown |\n| **Signing** | Did this identity really produce this exact operation? | Signature verification |\n| **Authorization** | Is this identity allowed to perform this action here? | Document permissions |\n| **Validity** | Is the resulting state legal? | The reducer |\n\n## What a signature does and does not prove\n\nA signature answers \"who, and unchanged?\" \u2014 authenticity plus integrity. It binds the identity to this specific operation *and its position in the log*, via the stamped `prevOpHash` and `prevOpIndex`.\n\nIt does **not** prove:\n\n- that the signer had permission on that document \u2014 that is a separate check against document permissions;\n- that the change is legal \u2014 the reducer still decides;\n- that this is the most recent operation \u2014 ordering comes from the index and hash chain, not the signature itself.\n\nSo a signed operation can be perfectly authentic and still be **rejected**, because the signer lacks permission on that document or because the reducer's rules do not allow the change. Identity establishes *trust*; it does not replace the other checks.\n\n## Why this structure is cheap\n\nNone of it needs a bolt-on audit system. Because state is already an ordered, hash-linked log of operations, attaching a signature to each entry is enough to make the whole history **tamper-evident**: change any past operation and every signature and hash after it stops verifying. The same structure that gives deterministic replay and conflict-free sync yields a trustworthy audit trail for free.\n\n> Doc note: the academy section is headed \"Three gates, not one\" but its table lists four (Authentication, Signing, Authorization, Validity). Four is what the table and the surrounding prose actually describe.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/02-Learn/08-deep-dives/02-identity-and-signing.mdx (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:35:17.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:35:17.000Z"
    }
  },
